# Cache rules for Cloudflare Workers static assets.
#
# This file exists because of a real failure, not as a precaution.
#
# After a deploy, the enquiry page died with "Application error: a client-side
# exception has occurred" and came back only on a hard reload. Nothing was
# wrong with the code. The browser was holding a cached copy of the HTML from
# the previous deploy, and that HTML names JavaScript chunks by content hash —
# /_next/static/chunks/main-a1b2c3.js. Those exact filenames no longer exist
# after a rebuild, so the page loaded, asked for scripts that 404'd, and threw.
#
# The visitor sees a dead page. A hard reload fixes it, but no customer does
# that; they leave. And it would have happened to every returning visitor on
# every deploy, which for a site that will be edited weekly is not a corner
# case.
#
# The fix is the standard split, and it works because of the hashes:
#
#   HTML   — never trusted from cache. Always revalidated, so a visitor always
#            learns which chunk filenames are current. It is a few KB.
#   Hashed
#   assets — cached hard, for a year, immutable. Safe precisely because the
#            filename changes whenever the content does: a new build produces
#            a new name, so a stale copy is never consulted.
#
# Net effect: the HTML is re-checked (usually a 304, a few bytes), and
# everything heavy still comes from cache. Pages stay fast and stop breaking.

/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Content-hashed by Next.js — the name IS the version.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Photographs and clips are not content-hashed: a filename like
# cricket-box-net.jpg stays the same when the picture behind it is replaced.
# A day is the compromise — long enough that repeat visitors and every page of
# a browsing session reuse them, short enough that swapping a product photo
# shows up the same day rather than whenever a browser decides to look again.
/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800
